{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DeALuZM3DdLmysHYyAGVst","type":"tool_result","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"selected_prompt_family\": \"paper_standalone\",\n  \"recommended_title\": \"Six Cheap Statistics versus Worst-Case Bounds: Predicting Random-Walk Cover Times on Small Graphs\",\n  \"central_question\": \"For connected graphs with at most 30 vertices, can a regression on six cheap graph statistics (n, m, mean/max/min degree, diameter) predict the expected cover time more accurately than the classical bounds 2m(n-1) and (4/27)n^3, and how does the error depend on the graph family?\",\n  \"central_thesis\": \"A log-linear ridge regression on six cheap statistics, fitted on Monte Carlo labels, predicts held-out isomorphism-disjoint small-graph cover times with mean absolute log error 0.183 (95% CI 0.167-0.207) against 1.397 for the better of the two generic bounds. Its advantage is significant in 9 of 10 families, is largest where the bounds are loosest (complete, Erdos-Renyi, random 3-regular), and is not statistically clear on the lollipop family, where the cubic bound is nearly tight; the predictor's own residual error is highest on complete, grid and Erdos-Renyi graphs.\",\n  \"narrative_rationale\": \"The only sealed final evidence is a completed, qualified evaluation of one predictor (the frozen selection, which is the original log-linear ridge configuration; the search produced no qualifying improved variant). That evidence answers the task's two questions directly: (i) accuracy against generic bounds, with paired two-level bootstrap CIs at plane and family level, and (ii) per-family reliability and failure. The bound-slack-by-family pattern gives a mechanistic reading: worst-case bounds are tight only near their extremal families (lollipop, path), so a fitted predictor gains most where the true cover time is far from the worst case. Evaluator controls (permuted-feature predictions, reordered-copy invariance, isomorphism-disjoint splits, closed-form simulator checks) let the paper make calibrated claims. A method-novelty story is unsupported, and so is a search-improvement story, because no new variant was scored. A pure negative-results story would misrepresent a qualified, strongly positive measurement. The strongest honest frame is therefore an empirical characterization study: a simple, reproducible predictor, controlled comparisons, and a family-resolved map of where it is reliable and where it is not.\",\n  \"prior_paper_story_status\": \"rejected\",\n  \"edit_framing_decision\": \"Edit mode is not active: there is no previous_paper/ directory, edit-context file or staged implementation. No prior story exists to preserve, broaden or recenter, so the framing is built fresh from the final evaluation. 'rejected' only records that no prior framing is carried over.\",\n  \"staged_input_impact\": [\n    \"No staged prior manuscript or new program inputs exist (no previous_paper/, aggregated/ or extra_tasks/). The framing rests only on paper_data.json final_evaluation, downstream_study, the frozen program source and the evaluator source.\",\n    \"downstream_study: selection_kind=baseline and investigation_outcome=completed_no_winner, with 1 attempt (0 scored, 1 unscored). The studied method is the original log-linear ridge configuration. The unscored model variant is missing evidence and is not part of any comparison.\",\n    \"final_evaluation: outcome=qualified, status=completed. All reported validity diagnostics are clean (iso_overlap_count 0, order_invariance_max_rel_diff 0, capped_walks 0, failed_episodes 0). The qualification therefore supports the measured-accuracy claims within the evaluated families and sizes.\"\n  ],\n  \"method_groups\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Statistics-only log-linear ridge predictor (studied method)\",\n      \"role\": \"proposed\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This is the frozen, sealed-evaluated predictor. It regresses ln(cover time) on the logs of the six statistics plus a ln n * ln diameter interaction, standardized, with ridge 1e-3 and an unpenalized intercept. Present it as a simple reproducible predictor, not a novel architecture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Generic analytic upper bounds used as predictors: 2m(n-1), (4/27)n^3, and their pointwise minimum\",\n      \"role\": \"baseline\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"These are the task's named comparator and the main answer to the research question. The pointwise minimum ('best bound') is the strongest bound-based predictor. Its paired plane-level and per-family delta CIs exist.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Matthews-type reference: exact maximum hitting time x H_{n-1}\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"This reference is a much tighter bound but needs the full graph, not cheap statistics. It shows the statistics-only predictor beats even a graph-aware bound on average (point estimates only; no paired CI is available).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Fitted reference predictors: OLS on (ln n, ln m) and a constant (geometric-mean training label)\",\n      \"role\": \"ablation\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The OLS(ln n, ln m) reference isolates the value of the degree and diameter statistics beyond size alone (paired delta CI available). The constant sets a floor for what fitting without features achieves. Neither is a training-matched ablation of the studied predictor; describe both as evaluator reference predictors.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"name\": \"Permuted-feature control\",\n      \"role\": \"supporting\",\n      \"include_reason\": \"The same fitted predictor is applied to deranged feature rows. The error rises to 1.261 (paired delta CI 0.955-1.196), showing that the predictions depend on each graph's own statistics.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"results_focus\": [\n    \"Plane-level accuracy: predictor 0.183 [0.167, 0.207] mean absolute log error vs best bound 1.397, Feige leading term 1.604, AKLLR 1.836, Matthews reference 0.605, OLS(ln n, ln m) 0.526, constant 0.942. Paired delta vs best bound CI [-1.303, -1.130]; paired delta vs OLS CI [-0.391, -0.288].\",\n    \"Per-family reliability map: predictor error ranges from 0.056 (star) to 0.381 (Erdos-Renyi). Low-error families are star, random 3-regular, random tree and path (<=0.1). Highest error is on complete 0.357, grid 0.364 and Erdos-Renyi 0.381.\",\n    \"Per-family paired gain over best bound: largest on random 3-regular (-2.09), complete (-2.08) and Erdos-Renyi (-2.07). It is significant in 9 of 10 families. Lollipop: -0.111, CI [-0.225, 0.013], which includes zero.\",\n    \"Mechanistic reading via bound slack (best-bound error = predictor error minus delta, derived per family): slack is smallest on lollipop (~0.28) and path (~0.48), the families near the bounds' worst case, and largest on complete/Erdos-Renyi (~2.4). Gains track slack.\",\n    \"Validity and uncertainty: permuted-feature control, order invariance 0, isomorphism overlap 0, no capped walks, median Monte Carlo relative SE 0.033, two-level bootstrap (graphs within family plus walks). Seed range 0 reflects a deterministic predictor on fixed labels, not robustness across data draws.\",\n    \"Limits on statistics-only prediction: 12 held-out graphs share their exact six-statistic vector with another graph, so any statistics-only predictor must give them identical predictions.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_plan\": [\n    \"Fig. 1 (page 1-2, full width): a concept overview. Ten synthetic graph families give a six-statistic vector. A log-linear ridge model produces a predicted cover time, set beside the two generic bounds. The evaluation side shows Monte Carlo labels, isomorphism-disjoint held-out graphs and paired bootstrap.\",\n    \"Fig. 2 (full width, 2 panels): per-family reliability map. (a) Dot plot of the predictor's mean absolute log error and the best bound's error per family, families sorted by bound slack. (b) Paired delta vs best bound with 95% bootstrap CI and a zero line, so the lollipop interval crossing zero is visible.\",\n    \"Fig. 3 (full width or ~0.8 textwidth, 2 panels): plane-level comparison. (a) Fitted/statistics predictors: studied predictor with CI, OLS(ln n, ln m), constant, permuted-feature control. (b) Bound-based predictors: best bound, (4/27)n^3, 2m(n-1), Matthews reference.\",\n    \"Table 1: plane-level errors and paired-delta CIs for every comparator. Table 2: the family roster with held-out counts derived from the evaluator catalogue.\"\n  ],\n  \"figure_specs\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"overview_concept\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"purpose\": \"Show the problem, the predictor and the evaluation protocol at a glance: graph families -> six cheap statistics -> log-linear ridge -> predicted cover time vs generic bounds, scored on isomorphism-disjoint held-out graphs with Monte Carlo labels and paired bootstrap.\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n      \"metric\": \"none (schematic)\",\n      \"split_by\": \"none\",\n      \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n      \"target_width_fraction\": 0.95,\n      \"layout_rationale\": \"The ICLR template is single column with a 5.5 in text width. A left-to-right pipeline with smal\nsource_evidence:\ndownstream\n\nsource_evidence/downstream:\nbaseline\nprogram\n\nsource_evidence/downstream/baseline:\nprogram_spec.toml\nsrc\n\nsource_evidence/downstream/baseline/src:\nprogram.py\n\nsource_evidence/downstream/program:\nprogram_spec.toml\nsrc\n\nsource_evidence/downstream/program/src:\nprogram.py\ncapture.py\nevaluate.py\nevaluate_final.py\nprogram_0\nrequirements.txt\nresources\ntask.md\ntask_contract.json\n\n## 2026-10-27T18:40:00Z reference_query_plan\n- Wrote reference_query_plan.{json,md} (status=ready, 10 queries).\n- Grounding: final model = ridge_log_linear_v0 baseline (main log err 0.183). Comparat