s ten families. You may translate 0.183 to a geometric-mean multiplicative error of about exp(0.183) \\u2248 1.20 and 1.397 to about 4.0. Say 'geometric-mean ratio', not 'percent error'.\",\n \"Qualification: the sealed final evaluation completed with outcome 'qualified' (final.qualification.*). Report the validity checks that back it: isomorphism-disjoint held-out graphs (overlap 0), order invariance (0), no capped walks, no failed episodes, closed-form simulator checks. Do not use pipeline labels for this; describe the checks themselves.\",\n \"Uncertainty: report the plane CI [0.167, 0.207] and the paired-delta CIs. The three evaluation episodes (seeds 211-213) give identical errors (seed range 0) because the predictor is deterministic and labels are fixed. Say explicitly that this is not variability across data draws; all uncertainty comes from the two-level bootstrap. The median Monte Carlo relative SE of the labels is 0.033.\",\n \"Lollipop: the paired delta CI [-0.225, 0.013] includes zero. Write 'no statistically clear advantage', not 'worse'. Interpret it through bound tightness: lollipop graphs are the extremal family for the cubic bound (state this as background, with a citation only if the bib supports it).\",\n \"Per-family best-bound errors are derived as predictor error minus delta. Label them 'derived' in figure captions and tables. Per-family CIs for the predictor's own error are not reported, so use the delta CIs only.\",\n \"High-error families (complete 0.357, grid 0.364, Erdos-Renyi 0.381): any explanation, such as a different growth regime that one log-linear surface cannot capture, or few training graphs per deterministic family, must be labelled a hypothesis. No ablation tests it.\",\n \"OLS(ln n, ln m) reference: it is evaluator-defined and differs from the studied predictor mainly in the feature set. Call it a size-only reference that indicates the value of the degree and diameter statistics, not a controlled ablation of the studied predictor.\",\n \"Matthews reference (0.605) needs exact hitting times from the full adjacency, so it is not a cheap statistic. Only point estimates exist, with no paired CI. Avoid significance language for this comparison.\",\n \"Feature collisions: 12 held-out graphs share an exact six-statistic vector with another graph. This is an inherent ambiguity for statistics-only predictors. Which families are affected is not reported, so do not guess.\",\n \"Selection scope: no improved variant was scored (1 attempt, 0 scored, 1 uns\nfailure_reason \"\"","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"75e1d124-5f7f-4219-805b-1d4a5db7e7d1","uuid":"2054a796-4293-4baf-ab19-270ba2a84199","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:29:44.359Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"status \"ready\"\nselected_prompt_family \"paper_standalone\"\nrecommended_title \"Six Cheap Statistics versus Worst-Case Bounds: Predicting Random-Walk Cover Times on Small Graphs\"\nprior_paper_story_status \"rejected\"\nfigure_specs [\n {\n  \"figure_id\": \"overview_concept\",\n  \"required\": true,\n  \"purpose\": \"Show the problem, the predictor and the evaluation protocol at a glance: graph families -> six cheap statistics -> log-linear ridge -> predicted cover time vs generic bounds, scored on isomorphism-disjoint held-out graphs with Monte Carlo labels and paired bootstrap.\",\n  \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n  \"preferred_generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n  \"group_by\": \"pipeline_stage\",\n  \"metric\": \"none (schematic)\",\n  \"split_by\": \"none\",\n  \"preferred_span\": \"full_width\",\n  \"target_width_fraction\": 0.95,\n  \"layout_rationale\": \"The ICLR template is single column with a 5.5 in text width. A left-to-right pipeline with small graph-family glyphs needs the full width so stage labels stay at or above 8 pt. Place it at the top of page 2, or page 1 if space allows.\",\n  \"allow_progression\": false,\n  \"max_panels\": 1,\n  \"max_categories_per_panel\": 6,\n  \"max_series_per_pane