{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTE8jU3DQH8Vf3gWehQn","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HxYqftUYnw1xGwVEvr6gXF","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\np='paper.tex';s=open(p).read()\nreps=[\n(r\"\\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWO} showed\", r\"\\citet{Aleliunas1979RandomWU} showed\"),\n(r\"Feige later showed that the worst case over all $n$-vertex graphs is $(4/27)n^3$ to leading order, attained asymptotically by lollipop graphs, and that every graph needs at least about $n\\ln n$ steps \\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\",\n r\"Feige later showed that the worst case over all $n$-vertex graphs is $(4/27)n^3$ to leading order, attained asymptotically by lollipop graphs, and that every connected graph needs at least about $n\\ln n$ steps \\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\"),\n(r\"The bound is within a constant factor of the truth for trees, but it overestimates dense graphs:\", r\"The bound overestimates dense graphs badly:\"),\n(r\"Feige's upper bound $\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case over $n$-vertex graphs and is approached by lollipops, i.e.\\ a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices joined to a path. Its matching lower bound $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$ is approached by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\",\n r\"Feige's upper bound $\\tfrac{4}{27}n^3+o(n^3)$ is the exact worst case over $n$-vertex graphs and is approached by lollipops, i.e.\\ a clique of about $2n/3$ vertices joined to a path. At the other extreme, every connected graph has cover time at least $(1-o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, which the complete graph approaches \\citep{Feige1995ATL}.\"),\n(r\"their cover times differ by roughly a factor of $n/\\ln n$.\", r\"their cover times differ by a factor that grows roughly like $n/\\ln n$.\"),\n(r\"The evaluator verified that the held-out pool has zero isomorphic overlap with the training pool.\", r\"The evaluator verified zero isomorphic overlap between the held-out pool and both the training and development pools.\"),\n(r\"no held-out or training walk reached the cap. The median relative standard error of the labels is 0.033.\", r\"no training or held-out walk reached the cap. The median relative standard error of the held-out labels is 0.033.\"),\n(r\"Their cover time is known to carry additional logarithmic factors that a single log-linear surface may not reproduce.\", r\"Their cover time may not follow a single power law in these statistics at this scale, which a log-linear surface cannot reproduce.\"),\n(r\"Twelve held-out graphs share their exact six-statistic vector with another graph.\", r\"Twelve held-out graphs share their exact six-statistic vector with at least one other graph in the training or held-out pool.\"),\n(r\"Non-isomorphic graphs with equal $(n,m,\\bar d, d_{\\max}, d_{\\min},\\diam)$ arise naturally among random trees and random 3-regular graphs.\", r\"Non-isomorphic graphs with equal $(n,m,\\bar d, d_{\\max}, d_{\\min},\\diam)$ can arise, for example, among trees with equal size, maximum degree and diameter.\"),\n(r\"took about 0.6, 1.2 and 1.2 seconds of candidate time respectively.\", r\"had recorded episode durations of about 0.6, 1.2 and 1.2 seconds respectively.\"),\n(r\"and 12 held-out graphs share an exact feature vector with another graph.\", r\"and 12 held-out graphs share an exact feature vector with another training or held-out graph.\"),\n(r\"zero isomorphic overlap between held-out and reference pools;\", r\"zero isomorphic overlap among the training, development and held-out pools;\"),\n]\nfor a,b in reps:\n    assert a in s, a[:60]\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ncat .claude/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md | sed -n 1,80p"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1735,"cache_read_input_tokens":128236,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1735,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens"