{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YWLsRF38ePw5r5F92oBA6K","type":"tool_result","content":"39:The expected cover time of a simple random walk is a basic quantity in graph exploration, yet the classical general-purpose upper bounds, $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$, can be loose on concrete small graphs. We ask whether a few cheap graph statistics, namely the number of vertices and edges, the mean, maximum and minimum degree, and the diameter, are enough to predict the cover time of connected graphs with $5\\le n\\le 30$ more accurately than these bounds, and how the error depends on graph type. We fit an untuned log-linear ridge regression to Monte Carlo cover-time labels on 117 training graphs from ten synthetic families. We then score it on 116 held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, using a paired two-level bootstrap. Its family-weighted mean absolute log error is 0.183 (95\\\n111:Each column is standardized with the training mean and standard deviation; a standard deviation below $10^{-12}$ is replaced by 1. An unpenalized intercept is added. The coefficients solve the ridge system $(Z^\\top Z+\\lambda D)\\beta = Z^\\top \\ln \\hat{\\mathbf C}$ with $\\lambda=10^{-3}$, where $D$ is the identity with a zero in the intercept position. At test time the predictor returns $\\hat y = \\exp\\bigl(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta^\\top \\tilde z, -50, 50)\\bigr)$, where $\\tilde z$ is the standardized design vector with a leading 1. Non-finite values fall back to the mean training log label. The source is at \\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 45--83. The small ridge term mainly resolves an exact collinearity: $\\ln\\bar d = \\ln 2 + \\ln m - \\ln n$, so the design matrix is rank-deficient without it. The predictor ignores the Monte Carlo standard errors and uses no hyperparameter search, cross-validation or family information. Its predictions are a deterministic, pure function of the six statistics of each row.\n334:The predictor (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}) depends only on NumPy and exposes a \\texttt{handle(request)} function with \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{fit} and \\texttt{predict} operations. Evaluation is CPU-only. The three episodes, with reset seeds 211, 212 and 213, had recorded episode durations of about 0.6, 1.2 and 1.2 seconds respectively. All three produced the same main error, 0.183.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"75e1d124-5f7f-4219-805b-1d4a5db7e7d1","uuid":"66245d5a-4c9e-4648-a9b6-e4e215b3300f","timestamp":"2026-10-27T18:40:45.900Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"39:The expected cover time of a simple random walk is a basic quantity in graph exploration, yet the classical general-purpose upper bounds, $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$, can be loose on concrete small graphs. We ask whether a few cheap graph statistics, namely the number of vertices and edges, the mean, maximum and minimum degree, and the diameter, are enough to predict the cover time of connected graphs with $5\\le n\\le 30$ more accurately than these bounds, and how the error depends on graph type. We fit an untuned log-linear ridge regression to Monte Carlo cover-time labels on 117 training graphs from ten synthetic families. We then score it on 116 held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, using a paired two-level bootstrap. Its family-weighted mean absolute log error is 0.183 (95\\\n111:Each column is standardized with the training mean and standard deviation; a standard deviation below $10^{-12}$ is replaced by 1. An unpenalized intercept is added. The coefficients solve the ridge system $(Z^\\top Z+\\lambda D)\\beta = Z^\\top \\ln \\hat{\\mathbf C}$ with $\\lambda=10^{-3}$, where $D$ is the identity with a zero in the intercept position. At test time the predictor returns $\\hat y = \\exp\\bigl(\\mathrm{clip}(\\beta^\\top \\tilde z, -50, 50)\\bigr)$, where $\\tilde z$ is the standardized design vector with a leading 1. Non-finite values fall back to the mean training log label. The source is at \\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}, lines 45--83. The small ridge term mainly resolves an exact collinearity: $\\ln\\bar d = \\ln 2 + \\ln m - \\ln n$, so the design matrix is rank-deficient without it. The predictor ignores the Monte Carlo standard errors and uses no hyperparameter search, cross-validation or family information. Its predictions are a deterministic, pure function of the six statistics of each row.\n334:The predictor (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py}) depends only on NumPy and exposes a \\texttt{handle(request)} function with \\texttt{reset}, \\texttt{fit} and \\texttt{predict} operations. Evaluation is CPU-only. The three episodes, with reset seeds 211, 212 and 213, had recorded episode durations of about 0.6, 1.2 and 1.2 seconds respectively. All three produced the same main error, 0.183.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
