{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTEPXRfVuCP5mFJqQM9P","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LmUbnrmQXyEUu2y3bFwazP","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\np='paper.tex';s=open(p).read()\ni=s.index('\\\\begin{abstract}')+len('\\\\begin{abstract}\\n'); j=s.index('\\\\end{abstract}')\nnew=r\"\"\"The classical cover-time bounds $2m(n-1)$ and $(4/27)n^3$ hold for every graph but can be loose on concrete small graphs. We ask whether six cheap statistics, namely vertex and edge counts, mean, maximum and minimum degree, and diameter, predict the expected cover time of connected graphs with $5\\le n\\le 30$ more accurately, and how the error depends on graph type. An untuned log-linear ridge regression is fitted to Monte Carlo labels on 117 graphs from ten synthetic families. It is scored on 116 held-out graphs that are isomorphism-disjoint from the training graphs, with a paired two-level bootstrap. Its mean absolute log error is 0.183 (95\\% CI 0.167--0.207), a typical error factor of 1.20, against 1.397 (factor 4.0) for the better bound. The paired advantage is significant in 9 of 10 families and largest where the bounds are loosest (complete, Erd\\H{o}s--R\\'enyi, random 3-regular). On lollipops, where the cubic bound is nearly tight, the advantage is not statistically clear. Fitted prediction helps most far from the worst case, but it carries no guarantee outside the studied families and sizes.\n\"\"\"\ns=s[:i]+new+s[j:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\na=s[s.index('begin{abstract}'):s.index('end{abstract}')];print(len(a.split()))\nPY\nbash $TMPDIR/build.sh 2>&1 | grep Output; PYTHONPATH=$TMPDIR/pylib python3 -c \"\nimport pymupdf as f,os;d=f.open(os.environ['TMPDIR']+'/build/paper.pdf')\nfor i in [0,2,5,6,8]: d[i].get_pixmap(dpi=80).save(os.environ['TMPDIR']+f'/build/q{i+1}.png')\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":nul